 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- If a slice contains no solid voxels, the entire slice will be filled [S1, unofficial].
-- If a slice is entirely solid, no voxels will be filled in that slice [S1, unofficial].
-- Precision issues may arise if the mesh bounds are not aligned with the voxel grid [G].
-- The algorithm assumes that the voxel grid is already populated with surface voxels from a previous step [G].
+- If a slice contains no solid voxels, the entire slice is filled [S1, unofficial].
+- If a slice is entirely solid, the scan encounters a solid voxel immediately and no voxels are filled in that slice [S1, unofficial].
+- The algorithm assumes the voxel grid is already populated with surface voxels from a preceding step [G].
+- Precision issues may arise if mesh bounds are not aligned with the voxel grid [G].
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Parallel Solid Task Execution
-Parallel Solid Task Execution extends simple slice filling by running the filling tasks in parallel using a thread pool [S1, unofficial]. It creates a thread pool with `num_thread` threads and submits the filling tasks for each slice along the x, y, and z axes to the thread pool [S1, unofficial]. It differs from simple slice filling by utilizing multi-threading to speed up the filling process [S1, unofficial]. It is chosen when performance is critical and the hardware supports multi-threading [S1, unofficial]. The implementation uses a `ThreadPool` class that supports `Run()` and `Stop()` methods [S1, unofficial]. Two variants are provided: `RunSolidTask` iterates x from lx to ux submitting FillYZ(x), y from ly to uy submitting FillXZ(y), and z from lz to uz submitting FillXY(z) [S1, unofficial]. `RunSolidTask2` iterates x from lx to ux submitting FillYZ2(x), z from lz to uz submitting FillXY2(z), and y from ly to uy submitting FillXZ2(y) [S1, unofficial].
+Parallel Solid Task Execution extends simple slice filling by running filling tasks in parallel using a thread pool [S1, unofficial]. It creates a `ThreadPool` with `num_thread` threads and submits slice filling tasks for each slice along the X, Y, and Z axes to the pool [S1, unofficial]. Two variants are provided: `RunSolidTask` iterates X from `lx` to `ux` submitting `FillYZ(x)`, Y from `ly` to `uy` submitting `FillXZ(y)`, and Z from `lz` to `uz` submitting `FillXY(z)` [S1, unofficial]. `RunSolidTask2` iterates X from `lx` to `ux` submitting `FillYZ2(x)`, Z from `lz` to `uz` submitting `FillXY2(z)`, and Y from `ly` to `uy` submitting `FillXZ2(y)` [S1, unofficial]. The thread pool supports `Run()` and `Stop()` methods [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Solid voxelization via XOR and 2D scan-filling
+An approach relying on surface voxelization and consequent 2D scan-filling in all three directions to acquire interior voxels [S2, unofficial]. It encodes binary voxels in separate bits of multiple targets, enabling processing many slices in a single pass [S2, unofficial]. A known pitfall is that only binary voxelization is generated due to XOR operations, and some voxels can be missed [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-- Time Complexity: O(N) where N is the number of voxels in the mesh voxelization range [G]. The parallel version reduces the wall-clock time by a factor proportional to the number of threads, assuming no contention [G].
